WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
Suppose a Hadamard matrix
has order n greater than one.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.100
We can choose two distinct rows,
and their dot product is zero.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.260
Each coordinate contributes
positive or negative one,

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.100
because both entries are signs.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.580
Our goal is to show that the number
n of contributions must be even.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.580
Use our known order four example.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.560
The two rows are negative
one, one, one, one;

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.150
and one, negative one, one, one.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.220
Multiply the entries vertically,
one coordinate at a time.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.295
The four products are negative
one, negative one, one, one.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.000
Separate the positive contributions
from the negative contributions.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.610
Pair each positive one
with a negative one.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.000
Our example gives two pairs,
and each pair sums to zero.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.090
An unpaired contribution would
prevent the total from being zero.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.700
Orthogonality requires the positive
and negative counts to match.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.360
Now count in general. Let a be the number
of positive products, and b the number of

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.980
negative products. The zero dot
product says a minus b equals zero.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.500
Counting all the positions
says a plus b equals n.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.140
Thus a equals b, so n equals a plus a,
which is two a. Therefore n is even.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.200
For a circulant matrix, the preceding
node also gives n equals m squared,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.860
with m a positive integer. We
have just proved that n is even.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.180
If m were odd, write m as two r plus one.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.080
Its square is four r squared plus
four r plus one, which is odd.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.960
That would contradict the evenness
of n. Therefore m is even.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:44.780
Write m equals two u,
with u a positive integer.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.913
Substituting into n equals m squared
gives n equals the square of two u,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.620
which is four u squared.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.030
The order four example
corresponds to u equal to one.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.180
We have reached n equals four u squared.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.640
We have not yet proved
that this same u is odd.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.340
That is the remaining
task for the next steps.
